---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';

// The downloadable PDF is this page printed to PDF (the print styles below
// lay it out for Letter paper). After editing this page, regenerate
// public/sean-sobieraj-resume.pdf so the two stay in sync.
const PDF_PATH = '/sean-sobieraj-resume.pdf';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title={`Resume · ${SITE_TITLE}`}
			description="Resume of Sean Sobieraj, cybersecurity data architect."
		/>
	</head>
	<body>
		<main class="resume">
			<header class="resume-head">
				<h1>Sean Sobieraj, CISSP</h1>
				<p class="title">Cybersecurity Data Architect</p>
				<p class="links">
					<a href="mailto:sean@sean.cc">sean@sean.cc</a>
					<span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
					<a href="/">sean.cc</a>
					<span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
					<a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/sobieraj/" rel="me"
						><span class="screen-only">LinkedIn</span><span class="print-only"
							>linkedin.com/in/sobieraj</span
						></a
					>
					<span class="screen-only" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
					<a class="screen-only" href={PDF_PATH}>Download PDF</a>
				</p>
			</header>

			<section>
				<h2>Summary</h2>
				<p>
					Nearly two decades in cybersecurity at CISA, the White House Situation Room, and US-CERT,
					after starting in network operations in the U.S. Marine Corps. Career centered on giving
					security data structure: from US-CERT's first machine-readable malware reports, through
					STIX-based information sharing, to CISA's enterprise data model and Open Cybersecurity
					Schema Framework (OCSF) telemetry standards. Now building on that work toward semantic
					modeling and knowledge graphs, where a shared model of security entities and their
					relationships lets analysts and AI systems reason across sources.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2>Experience</h2>

				<article class="role">
					<h3>Senior Data Architect</h3>
					<p class="meta">
						Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA)
						<span class="dates">2022–Present</span>
					</p>
					<ul>
						<li>
							Lead the Cybersecurity Division's data management initiative, setting its long-term
							roadmap, governance structure, and data stewardship model.
						</li>
						<li>
							Architected and guided the production deployment of an OCSF-aligned telemetry framework
							that continuously normalizes more than 100 feeds from over two dozen federal agencies
							into a common schema (more than three trillion events to date), enabling cross-source
							discovery and analysis.
						</li>
						<li>
							Simplified the enterprise data model from hundreds of classes to fewer than 10 intuitive
							domains, and introduced a domain-oriented architecture to reduce organizational silos.
						</li>
						<li>
							Defined agency-wide medallion architecture, tagging, and metadata practices, including
							governed tags for PII, PCII, TLP, and CUI, plus a framework covering provenance, sharing,
							and access control across layers.
						</li>
						<li>
							Designed core data infrastructure on Databricks, AWS, Alation, and Elasticsearch for
							batch and streaming workloads, including the division's first shared, reusable pipeline
							framework and validation rules that keep data trusted from raw to curated layers.
						</li>
						<li>
							Identified a gap in the OCSF DNS schema and advanced an accepted change proposal with
							external partners; developed guidance for using OCSF and STIX together.
						</li>
						<li>
							Developed standardized cyber threat intelligence and entity-reference architectures, and
							integrated indicator and agency-reference data into a threat-hunting proof of concept.
						</li>
						<li>
							Proposed a graph-based model to unify visibility across customers, services, and data
							assets.
						</li>
					</ul>
				</article>

				<article class="role">
					<h3>DevOps Team Lead, Threat Hunting</h3>
					<p class="meta">
						Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA)
						<span class="dates">2018–2022</span>
					</p>
					<ul>
						<li>
							Established and led a cross-functional development team of federal and contract staff,
							prototyping analytic tools for threat hunting.
						</li>
						<li>
							Automated threat intelligence preparation for incident response, replacing hours of manual work per engagement: led development of a MongoDB-backed platform that
							polled six external vendors and the malware triage sandbox on a schedule and produced
							Splunk-ready lookup tables for each response kit.
						</li>
						<li>
							Built on-premises data lake capabilities with MinIO and Dremio, integrated with cloud
							object storage, supporting many of the team's analytic tools.
						</li>
						<li>
							Introduced Git branching, CI/CD, and automated testing across the team, and supported
							rollout of an OpenShift Kubernetes cluster for the analytic environment.
						</li>
					</ul>
				</article>

				<article class="role">
					<h3>Technical Lead, Capability Engineering</h3>
					<p class="meta">
						DHS National Cybersecurity and Communications Integration Center (NCCIC)
						<span class="dates">2014–2018</span>
					</p>
					<ul>
						<li>
							Designed and built DHS's malware triage and reporting platform, cutting the time to first
							findings from days to minutes. It stayed in use for more than a decade and earned the DHS
							Office of Cybersecurity and Communications Innovation Award.
						</li>
						<li>
							Integrated more than 30 open-source, commercial, and custom components (including Cuckoo
							Sandbox, YARA, PostgreSQL, and Celery) into a single workflow producing analyst reports in
							six formats, including STIX XML.
						</li>
						<li>
							Created a PostgreSQL repository of eight years of malware report data, enabling
							correlation across incidents and automated trending and metrics.
						</li>
						<li>
							Replicated the platform at other DHS locations and provided it as the proof of concept for
							the Advanced Malware Analysis Center.
						</li>
						<li>
							Wrote a Python-STIX marking extension for DHS's Automated Indicator Sharing (AIS)
							capability, and guided other DHS components on implementing STIX, CybOX, and MAEC.
						</li>
					</ul>
				</article>

				<article class="role">
					<h3>Duty Officer</h3>
					<p class="meta">
						White House Situation Room, National Security Council
						<span class="dates">2013–2014</span>
					</p>
					<ul>
						<li>
							Provided continuous situational awareness to the President, Vice President, National
							Security Advisor, and senior White House officials on rotating 12-hour day and night
							shifts, monitoring all-source reporting and alerting on breaking crises.
						</li>
						<li>
							Wrote and peer-reviewed the President's daily summaries and National Security Council
							summaries of global events.
						</li>
						<li>Supported the President's and senior officials' calls with foreign heads of state.</li>
					</ul>
				</article>

				<article class="role">
					<h3>Malware Analysis Team Lead</h3>
					<p class="meta">
						U.S. Computer Emergency Readiness Team (US-CERT)
						<span class="dates">2008–2013</span>
					</p>
					<ul>
						<li>
							Led malware incident response, coordinating with analytic teams, law enforcement, and
							intelligence agencies.
						</li>
						<li>
							Created US-CERT's first machine-readable malware report format and standardized the
							report template adopted across US-CERT.
						</li>
						<li>
							Helped develop the database ontology, XML schema, and TLP guidelines that led to
							US-CERT's Cyber Indicators Analysis Platform.
						</li>
						<li>
							Developed the team's triage utility (Python) and report editor (JavaScript), automating
							analysis and reporting workflows.
						</li>
					</ul>
				</article>

				<article class="role">
					<h3>Sergeant, Network Operations</h3>
					<p class="meta">
						U.S. Marine Corps
						<span class="dates">2000–2004</span>
					</p>
					<ul>
						<li>
							Maintained the Camp Lejeune regional network (1,500 switches, 35,000 users); supervised
							three technicians and managed more than $500,000 in equipment.
						</li>
						<li>
							Built a fiber network from scratch for Joint Task Force 160 in Guantanamo Bay, Cuba,
							during Operation Enduring Freedom.
						</li>
					</ul>
				</article>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2>Education and Certification</h2>
				<ul class="plain">
					<li>
						<strong>M.S. Information Security</strong>, Purdue University (federal Scholarship for
						Service), 2008
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>B.S. Applied Networking and System Administration</strong>, summa cum laude,
						Rochester Institute of Technology, 2006
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>CISSP</strong>, Certified Information Systems Security Professional, (ISC)²
					</li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2>Skills</h2>
				<ul class="plain">
					<li>
						<strong>Data architecture:</strong> conceptual and domain data modeling, ontology and schema
						design, medallion architecture, metadata and tagging, data governance, data lineage
					</li>
					<li><strong>Standards:</strong> OCSF, STIX, MITRE ATT&amp;CK, ECS</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Platforms:</strong> Databricks (Unity Catalog, Spark Declarative Pipelines), AWS,
						Elasticsearch, PostgreSQL, MongoDB, Alation, Dremio, MinIO
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Engineering:</strong> Python, PySpark, SQL, JavaScript, REST APIs, Docker,
						Kubernetes, Git, CI/CD
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Security:</strong> malware analysis and reverse engineering, incident response
					</li>
				</ul>
			</section>
		</main>
		<div class="screen-only"><Footer /></div>
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	/* Screen: same column, type, and colors as the rest of the site. */
	.resume-head {
		margin-bottom: 2.5em;
	}
	h1 {
		font-size: 1.6em;
		margin-bottom: 0.15rem;
	}
	.title {
		margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
		font-size: 1.1em;
		color: rgb(var(--black));
	}
	.links {
		margin: 0;
		color: rgb(var(--gray));
		font-size: 0.9em;
	}
	.links > span {
		margin: 0 0.4em;
	}
	.print-only {
		display: none;
	}
	.links a {
		color: rgb(var(--gray));
		text-decoration: none;
		white-space: nowrap;
	}
	.links a:hover {
		color: var(--accent-hover);
		text-decoration: underline;
	}
	section {
		margin-bottom: 2.25em;
	}
	h2 {
		font-size: 0.8em;
		font-weight: 600;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		letter-spacing: 0.08em;
		color: rgb(var(--gray));
		padding-bottom: 0.4em;
		border-bottom: 1px solid rgb(var(--gray-light));
		margin-bottom: 1em;
	}
	section > p {
		margin: 0;
	}
	.role {
		margin-bottom: 1.75em;
	}
	.role:last-child {
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}
	h3 {
		font-size: 1em;
		margin: 0;
	}
	.meta {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		justify-content: space-between;
		column-gap: 1em;
		margin: 0 0 0.5em;
		color: rgb(var(--gray));
		font-size: 0.9em;
	}
	.dates {
		white-space: nowrap;
		font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
	}
	ul {
		margin: 0;
		padding-left: 1.2em;
	}
	li {
		margin-bottom: 0.45em;
	}
	li:last-child {
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}
	ul.plain {
		list-style: none;
		padding-left: 0;
	}

	/* Print / PDF: Letter paper, black on white whatever the screen theme,
	   tighter spacing so it fits on two pages. */
	@media print {
		@page {
			size: letter;
			margin: 0.45in 0.6in;
		}
		:global(:root) {
			color-scheme: light;
			--bg: 255, 255, 255;
			--black: 0, 0, 0;
			--gray-dark: 25, 25, 25;
			--gray: 85, 85, 85;
			--gray-light: 200, 200, 200;
			--accent: #000000;
		}
		:global(body) {
			background: #fff;
			font-size: 10pt;
			line-height: 1.35;
		}
		:global(main) {
			width: auto;
			max-width: none;
			padding: 0;
		}
		.screen-only {
			display: none;
		}
		.print-only {
			display: inline;
		}
		.resume-head {
			margin-bottom: 1.1em;
		}
		h1 {
			font-size: 1.75em;
		}
		.title {
			font-size: 1.1em;
			margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
		}
		section {
			margin-bottom: 1.1em;
		}
		h2 {
			margin-bottom: 0.6em;
			padding-bottom: 0.25em;
			break-after: avoid;
		}
		.role {
			margin-bottom: 0.85em;
		}
		h3,
		.meta {
			break-after: avoid;
		}
		.meta {
			margin-bottom: 0.25em;
		}
		li {
			margin-bottom: 0.2em;
			break-inside: avoid;
		}
	}
</style>
